{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PG4Zhe3HgVkk1oqh8hHvZf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose tasks.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a Go project that re-implements Unix utilities from specifications derived from C source code. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → write PRDs → generate Go code → verify via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge from reference behavior on edge cases.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - One package per utility (main.go + tests)\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n\n\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform-specific syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specs, PRDs, use cases\n\n**Current State**: The `cat` and `du` utilities are already implemented with their test suites, and `sponge` is partially in place. I'm seeing that all these packages already exist in the source code, so I shouldn't propose recreating them. Let me look at which releases actually need work by checking the release status.\n\nLooking at the releases in scope, I can see that release 00.0 covers the foundational infrastructure with four use cases—magefiles, format, sys, and testutils—all with complete specs but no code started yet. Release 01.0 focuses on the wc command, 01.1 on cat, and 01.2 appears to be cut off, but these also show specs complete with no implementation begun.\n\nWait, I'm noticing something important here: the source code directory already contains implementations for all these use cases. The code_status field says \"not started,\" but that's clearly stale information—the actual source code shows complete implementations across all these release