clarify that signal handling is an architecture-specified capability, then write out the task with the proper required reading list.\n\nI'm looking at the files that need changes: pkg/sys needs a new signal handler module to consolidate the SIGPIPE pattern currently duplicated across cmd/cat and cmd/du, and cmd/wc needs to adopt this shared handler to fulfill its R5 requirements. I'll create the signal handler in pkg/sys with tests, then update cmd/wc to use it. Now I need to replace those local signal handlers in cat and du with calls to the new shared function, removing the redundant imports. I should also add the same SIGPIPE handling to wc's run function since it currently lacks it. Then I'll need to write tests for the new InstallSIGPIPE function to verify it properly handles the signal and exits. be called without panic and that the signal channel is registered. I'm using the existing test patterns from terminal_test.go and fileinfo_test.go as a reference.\n\nThe function should be named InstallSIGPIPE to match the verb pattern in the cmd/ callers, following Go conventions with the Install prefix and SIGPIPE in all-caps. Since syscall.SIGPIPE and os/signal are portable across Darwin and Linux, I don't need platform-specific build tags like I would for fileinfo. The handler should exit with code 0 when SIGPIPE is received, matching the existing behavior in cmd/cat and cmd/du—this is the standard Unix convention for silent termination on broken pipes.\n\nNow I'm checking the acceptance criteria to make sure I'm covering all the requirements. There are seven items total: the signal.go and signal_test.go files need to exist and export the function, cmd/cat and cmd/du need to stop using their local handlers and call sys.InstallSIGPIPE instead, cmd/wc needs to add the call at the start of run(), and all builds and tests need to pass. That's a solid set of requirements that aligns with the P9 guidance of 5-8 acceptance criteria.\n\nLet me verify the structure against